The Thetanuts index vault lets holders `claim()` a proportional basket of underlying component-vault shares (BTC/ETH/AVAX/BNB/MATIC USD vaults) for their index shares, and `mint()` new index shares by depositing component shares. The accounting is broken: an attacker can claim out the component shares and then re-mint replacement index shares while the component-vault transfer amounts pulled by mint stay at zero (the mint does not actually charge/transfer the component shares it should). The attacker flash-loans nearly the entire index-share balance from Aave's aToken, claims the component shares from the borrowed index shares, then loops `mint()` to fabricate enough index shares to repay the Aave flash loan - at no real cost. After repaying the loan, the claimed component vault shares (later converted to USDC via initWithdraw) plus residual shares are pure profit, ~105,471 USDC.

**Vulnerable code snippet:**

```solidity
// step 2: claim component shares from borrowed index shares
indexVault.claim(borrowedShares);
// step 3: remint index shares to repay Aave while component transfer amounts stay 0
while (indexVault.balanceOf(address(this)) < repaymentShares) {
    uint256 supplyBoundedMint = indexVault.totalSupply() - 1;
    uint256 mintAmount = remaining < supplyBoundedMint ? remaining : supplyBoundedMint;
    indexVault.mint(mintAmount);
}
```

