Sharwa Finance's margin trading system failed to properly enforce solvency on a margin account across the increase/decrease long position flow. Using a Morpho flash loan of USDC, the attacker created a margin account, supplied liquidity to the SF LP pool, provided collateral, and opened a large WBTC long via `increaseLongPosition`. In a second transaction (next block) the attacker flash-loaned WBTC, swapped it on Uniswap V3 to skew accounting, and called `decreaseLongPosition` with nearly the full position size. Because the position/collateral accounting and the post-trade insolvency check were inadequate (the check happened in a way the attacker could game across the swap and position-decrease), the attacker was able to withdraw more value than they put in, draining ~146K USD in WBTC. The flaw is a missing/incorrect post-operation insolvency validation on the margin account.

**Vulnerable code snippet:**

```solidity
// increase then decrease a leveraged long with no sound post-trade solvency check
tradeRouter.increaseLongPosition(marginAccountID, address(WBTC), 36_200_000);
// ... next tx, after manipulating balances via V3 swaps ...
tradeRouter.decreaseLongPosition(marginAccountID, address(WBTC), 36_199_999);
```

